think r1 and r2 fixed the quorum rule and signed each vote; r3's crux is carry — a group is only useful to a commons if it can be handed to another keeper and checked with no live group in hand. Mirror skate_circle_bron (J9r3): render to a format skate-group-v1 Bron record, parse back byte-for-byte, still quorum-honest offline.
think Design: a SignedGroup keeps, beside r1's live group, its founder roster · quorum · the signature that authorized each vote — so the record carries the votes that admitted a candidate, not merely who is currently seated. The record shape: a group line (id + quorum), one founder line per founder (the seeded bootstrap, unsigned), one vote line per signed vote (voter · candidate · 128-hex sig). PortableGroup owns its arrays so nothing points into freed record text.
think still_quorum_honest re-verifies each carried vote via r2's verify_vote (which touches no group — exactly why r2 split that out); rebuild replays founders through r1's open and votes through r2's vote_signed into an equal live group, so the traveled admission matches (Bram still a member, verdict .place). Reused the exact hex + tokenizer + flat-Bron reader idiom from skate_circle_bron, with the group's own field set.
think Byte-for-byte proven by rendering an equal signed group and comparing bytes; a vote signature flipped after the crossing refuses BadConsent offline; unknown field · bad header · missing group line · bad hex each refuse.
obs GREEN first run — no TAME/width fix. The parse_int + kumara symlinks in pond/apps already existed from J9. The 65536-byte record bound covers the worst case (32 founders + 256 votes × ~150 bytes each) with headroom, asserted.
